From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2_codec2: size decoder input buffers for the stream again

LineageOS raised the input buffer size for streams up to 1080p from
2 MiB to 16 MiB (e5ee3bf, "Raise the input buffer size for 1080p to
16MB") because the Qualcomm iris driver sized every input buffer for a
4096x2304 frame (6.75 MiB, 13.5 MiB for VP9) and rejected anything
smaller. Above 1080p the size is four times that. A session keeps up to
16 of these buffers and maps them through the decoder as they are
queued, so a 1080p session used about 275 MiB of decoder address space
and a 1440p one about 735 MiB. A web page with a few looping videos
then needs gigabytes.

The iris driver on this device now keeps the size the client sets on
the OUTPUT queue, down to half a 4:2:0 frame at 720p (a whole one for
VP9), see patches/kernel/iris/0019. Go back to 2 MiB up to 1080p and
8 MiB above, as the comment above the constant still describes. Coded
frames are far smaller: the largest one in a 7 Mbps 1080p H.264 stream
is 673 KB, in a 28 Mbps 4K HEVC stream 204 KB. A larger max-input-size
set by the client is kept by MaxInputBufferSizeCalculator, as before.
The driver's lower bound does not get in the way for any codec:
setupInputFormat() passes the device's minimum resolution to S_FMT in
this tree, so the bound stays at its 720p floor (about 1.35 MiB for VP9,
whose bound is a whole frame rather than half of one).

The size is chosen when the component is configured. A session set up
at up to 1080p now takes coded frames of at most 2 MiB, also after the
stream switches to a higher resolution, as before e5ee3bf; VP9 above
1080p takes at most 8 MiB instead of 13.5 MiB. Clients size their input
buffers from the codec's max-input-size: Chromium re-creates the codec
with larger buffers when a key frame does not fit, and Media3 sizes them
for the largest format of an adaptive stream.

Also check the sizeimage the driver returns. A driver that needs more
than the allocated size rejects the input buffers at VIDIOC_QBUF, so
setupInputFormat() now fails at once and logs both sizes.

Checked on the Huawei MateBook E Go with patches/kernel/iris/0019: input
buffers are mapped at 2 MiB, 8 MiB for 4K clips. H.264 (480p, 1080p,
4K), HEVC 1080p and VP9 (1080p, 4K) decode through the byte-buffer and
Surface paths with output identical to the software decoders, as do two
H.264 streams that change resolution midway. On minimax.cn, where Edge
opened 316 decoder sessions during 12 scroll cycles (78 of them 1440p),
a 1080p session used about 150 MiB and a 1440p one about 290 MiB, and
the decoder's mappings peaked at about 1.5 GiB instead of about
2.4 GiB, with no failed buffer. On a kernel without iris/0019, 1080p
H.264 and VP9 fail at start with "Driver needs 7077888-byte input
buffers, larger than the 2097152 bytes allocated."

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [needs the iris input size change in patches/kernel/iris/0019]
---
 components/DecodeInterface.cpp | 2 +-
 v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp           | 9 +++++++++
 2 files changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/components/DecodeInterface.cpp b/components/DecodeInterface.cpp
index 3d751d3..0397deb 100644
--- a/components/DecodeInterface.cpp
+++ b/components/DecodeInterface.cpp
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@ constexpr size_t k4KArea = 3840 * 2160;
 // when using higher bitrates, like 1Mb/s on slower devices. Also, this brings up compability
 // with the Chrome browser as it is using 2MB buffer size for a 1080p stream, ref:
 // https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:media/gpu/gpu_video_encode_accelerator_helpers.cc;l=25
-constexpr size_t kInputBufferSizeFor1080p = 16 * 1024 * 1024;  // 16MB
+constexpr size_t kInputBufferSizeFor1080p = 2 * 1024 * 1024;  // 2MB
 // Input bitstream buffer size for up to 4k streams.
 constexpr size_t kInputBufferSizeFor4K = 4 * kInputBufferSizeFor1080p;
 
diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
index 1d595b9..24e98e5 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
@@ -220,6 +220,15 @@ bool V4L2Decoder::setupInputFormat(const uint32_t inputPixelFormat, const size_t
     }
     ALOG_ASSERT(format->fmt.pix_mp.pixelformat == inputPixelFormat);
 
+    // Input blocks are inputBufferSize bytes long unless the client allocates
+    // its own. A driver that needs larger buffers would reject them at
+    // VIDIOC_QBUF, so fail here instead of in the middle of decoding.
+    if (format->fmt.pix_mp.plane_fmt[0].sizeimage > inputBufferSize) {
+        ALOGE("Driver needs %u-byte input buffers, larger than the %zu bytes allocated.",
+              format->fmt.pix_mp.plane_fmt[0].sizeimage, inputBufferSize);
+        return false;
+    }
+
     if (mInputQueue->allocateBuffers(kNumInputBuffers, V4L2_MEMORY_DMABUF) == 0) {
         ALOGE("Failed to allocate input buffer.");
         return false;
